From 00a6f143b44b987ce060c151548984dcb020bb34 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Micheal Waltz <micheal@ecliptik.com>
Date: Tue, 22 Sep 2026 22:21:33 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] dos: patch 0192 -- SDL_DOSAudioSetContentActive: the SB
 silence gate keeps a sound's own silence

WHY: DOSSOUNDBLASTER_PlayDevice's post-mix silence gate (SDL/0054, "Shape A") drops every buffer
that is silent in the DEVICE format instead of writing it to the ring. Between sounds that is its
purpose (the ring drains; with idle-pause enabled the DSP pauses). During a sound it removes time.
dosags (PLAN.md step 8, UR5-UR8, 8.3 hardware round) measured both forms: (a) a clip's own
digital-zero pauses -- 5 Days' sound 9 is 52 of 88 windows exact zero -- are dropped, so on hardware
~50 silent IRQs per play were counted as dropouts and, with audio queued, the pauses shrink; (b) on
the 8-bit path (SDL's F32->U8 rounds |s| < 128 of 32768 to 0x80) every chunk quieter than ~-48 dBFS
is dropped too: sound 10 played ~9.0 s on the PicoGUS instead of ~9.76-10.0 s. Chunk accounting
was exact: pcm IRQs = chunks mixed - chunks silent at the device (16-bit 582 = 639 - 57; 8-bit
516 = 605 - 59 - 29). Judging silence before format conversion would not help: a chunk that rounds
to all 0x80 is also silent when played, so nothing audible is lost -- only TIME, the same defect as
(a). And "keep silence while the ring holds audio" cannot work: SDL3's playback iterate fills an
empty stream with silence and still calls PlayDevice (SDL_audio.c, br < buffer_size -> memset), so
after the first sound the ring would never empty and the gate would never skip again. What decides
it is whether content is playing, and only the port's mixer knows that.

WHAT: SDL_DOSAudioSetContentActive(bool) (SDL_dosaudio_pump.h). While true, PlayDevice skips the
silence scan and writes silent buffers like any other; the gate, its SDL_Delay(10) throttle and
idle-pause are untouched otherwise. Default false: a port that never calls it -- doskutsu and every
SDL_mixer port -- gets byte-identical behaviour. Two monotonic counters beside the other per-IRQ
audio counters, for the engine to report: dos_port_audio_skipped_silent_count (buffers the gate
dropped) and dos_port_audio_idle_pause_count (DSP idle-pause commands issued).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01Nz9XeKeVG8Ab7dm8esJsMu
